Notebook[
 {Cell["Lepton-quark scattering, step 4: the unpolarized NLO hard \
coefficient", "Title"], Cell["Input: Results/<route>/Algebra.wl (step 2) and \
Results/<route>/Reduced.wl (step 3).  Output: Results/<route>/Unpolarized.wl \
with the finite coefficient in the basis {regular, D0, D1, delta}, and its \
comparison with Hinderer, Schlegel and Vogelsang, arXiv:1505.06415.\n\nHard \
coefficient.  Following that paper we use sigma(s,v,w) = s E dsigma/d^3p for \
the partonic cross section, with t = -s(1-v), u = -s v w, s4 = s v (1-w).  \
Hadronic cross sections follow by convoluting sigma with the parton \
distribution and fragmentation function (see docs/CONVENTIONS.md).  At Born \
level the recoiling lepton fixes w = 1:\n\n   sigma_Born = |M|^2 / (16 Pi^2 s \
v) delta(1-w),\n\nwhere |M|^2 is the spin- and colour-averaged squared matrix \
element.  Real emission with the two-body phase space Phi2 of the unobserved \
pair gives sigma_real = (integral of |M|^2 over Phi2)/(32 Pi^3).  The \
couplings carry the scale muR^(2 eps) in D dimensions (MSbar), and the \
four-dimensional measure of the observed parton is common to every \
term.\n\nSteps: (1) Born and photon-quark Born through order eps; (2) real \
emission: separate the threshold branch (1-w)^(-1-2eps) and expand into plus \
distributions; (3) virtual correction; (4) check that the double and single \
poles cancel against the MSbar counterterms of the incoming quark, the \
outgoing quark and the photon in the lepton; (5) collect the finite \
coefficient and compare with the literature.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Load", "Section"], Cell["Get[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \
\"..\", \"..\", \"FeynFacet\", \"FeynFacet.wl\"}]]", "Input"], 
  Cell["route = \"FeynCalc\";\nalgebra = LoadResult[\"Algebra\", \
route];\nreduced = LoadResult[\"Reduced\", route];\nregion = s > 0 && muR > 0 \
&& 0 < v < 1 && 0 < w < 1;          (* physical region; fixes every \
logarithm's branch *)\ntoVW = {t -> -s (1 - v), s4 -> s v (1 - w), u -> -s v \
w};\ncouplings = {ee -> Sqrt[4 Pi alphaEM], gs -> Sqrt[4 Pi \
alphaS]};\nKeys[reduced]", "Input"], Cell["Born terms", "Section"], 
  Cell["The D-dimensional Born hard part, bornHard = born0 + eps born1 + ..., \
multiplies the collinear counterterms, so its order-eps term contributes to \
the finite result.  At Born level u = -s v (w = 1).", "Text"], 
  Cell["bornHard = Series[algebra[\"Born\"][\"Unpolarized\"]/(16 Pi^2 s v) /. \
D -> 4 - 2 eps /. couplings /. {t -> -s (1 - v), u -> -s v}, {eps, 0, \
1}];\nborn0 = SeriesCoefficient[bornHard, {eps, 0, 0}] // Simplify;\nborn1 = \
SeriesCoefficient[bornHard, {eps, 0, 1}] // Simplify;\nRequire[Simplify[born0 \
- 2 alphaEM^2 eq^2 (1 + v^2)/(s v (1 - v)^2)] === 0, \"Born hard part differs \
from Eq. (22) of HSV\"];\n{born0, born1}", "Input"], 
  Cell["Photon-quark Born gamma(q) + q(k) -> q(p) + g(r).  The photon carries \
the fraction y of the lepton momentum, so that sh = y s, th = -s(1-v), uh = \
-y s v w, and its on-shell condition sh + th + uh = 0 fixes y = (1-v)/(1-vw). \
 Its hard part photonHard = photon0 + eps photon1 is normalized like \
sigma_Born, with the Jacobian for the photon fraction: photonHard = |M|^2/(16 \
Pi^2 y s (1 - v w)) in units of alphaS/Pi.", "Text"], 
  Cell["yPhoton = (1 - v)/(1 - v w);\nphotonHard = Series[(Pi/alphaS) \
algebra[\"Photon\"][\"Unpolarized\"]/(16 Pi^2 yPhoton s (1 - v w)) /. D -> 4 \
- 2 eps /. couplings /.\n  {sh -> yPhoton s, th -> -s (1 - v), uh -> -yPhoton \
s v w}, {eps, 0, 1}];\nphoton0 = Simplify[SeriesCoefficient[photonHard, {eps, \
0, 0}], region];\nphoton1 = Simplify[SeriesCoefficient[photonHard, {eps, 0, \
1}], region];\nRequire[Simplify[photon0 - 2 Pi CF alphaEM eq^2 (1 + v^2 \
w^2)/(s (1 - v) v w)] === 0, \"Photon-quark Born differs from the expected \
form\"];\n{photon0, photon1}", "Input"], 
  Cell["Real emission and its threshold expansion", "Section"], 
  Cell["The integrated real contribution from step 3, in the variables v, w.  \
It contains the exact factors s4^(-eps) = (s v (1-w))^(-eps) and, from the AB \
family, zAB^(-1-eps) and zAB^(-eps) with zAB = (1-v)(1-w)/(1-v+vw).  Together \
they give the threshold branches (1-w)^(-1-2eps) and (1-w)^(-1-eps) that \
produce the plus distributions.  ThresholdSplit makes every power of (1-w) \
explicit (all other factors are positive in the physical region) and writes \
the result as\n\n   sigma_real = A(w, eps) + Sum over lambda of \
(1-w)^(-1-lambda eps) B_lambda(w, eps),\n\nwith A and B_lambda regular at w = \
1.", "Text"], Cell["realHard = muR^(2 eps) \
reduced[\"Real\"][\"Unpolarized\"]/(32 Pi^3) /. couplings /. toVW;\nsplit = \
ThresholdSplit[realHard, w, eps];\nKeys[split[\"Branches\"]]", "Input"], 
  Cell["EndpointExpand applies (1-w)^(-1-lambda eps) = -delta(1-w)/(lambda \
eps) + D0 - lambda eps D1 + ... to every branch, with D0 = [1/(1-w)]_+ and D1 \
= [log(1-w)/(1-w)]_+, and splits w-dependent coefficients into their value at \
w = 1 times the distribution plus an ordinary remainder.  The result has the \
double pole, the single poles in the basis {regular, D0, delta} and the \
finite part in {regular, D0, D1, delta}.", "Text"], 
  Cell["realExpanded = EndpointExpand[split[\"Regular\"], \
split[\"Branches\"], eps, w, region];\nSimplify[realExpanded[\"Pole2\"], \
region]", "Input"], Cell["Virtual correction", "Section"], 
  Cell["The one-loop vertex correction is the quark form factor times the \
Born term: 2 Re(M1 M0 / |M0|^2 = (alphaS CF/(2 Pi)) (4 Pi muR^2)^eps x \
virtual, where virtual is the reduced expression of step 3 with the massless \
bubble inserted, (-t)^(-eps) Gamma(eps) Gamma(1-eps)^2/Gamma(2-2eps).  Its \
Laurent expansion is -2/eps^2 - 3/eps - 8 + O(eps) times (4 Pi \
muR^2/(-t))^eps Gamma(1+eps) Gamma(1-eps)^2/Gamma(1-2eps), the standard \
result.  The external-leg graphs are scaleless and vanish; in the MSbar \
scheme their UV pole is cancelled by the wave-function counterterm and their \
IR pole is part of the collinear subtraction below.", "Text"], 
  Cell["virtualHard = Series[alphaS CF/(2 Pi) (4 Pi muR^2)^eps \
reduced[\"Virtual\"] (born0 + eps born1) /. t -> -s (1 - v), {eps, 0, \
0}];\nvirtualHard = Map[Simplify[#, region] &, virtualHard];\nvirtualPole2 = \
SeriesCoefficient[virtualHard, {eps, 0, -2}];\nvirtualPole1 = \
SeriesCoefficient[virtualHard, {eps, 0, -1}];\nvirtualFinite = \
SeriesCoefficient[virtualHard, {eps, 0, 0}];\nRequire[Simplify[virtualPole2 + \
2 alphaS CF/(2 Pi) born0] === 0, \"Virtual double pole is not -2 times the \
Born term\"];\nvirtualPole2", "Input"], Cell["Pole cancellation", "Section"], 
  Cell["The double poles of real and virtual terms (both proportional to \
delta(1-w)) must cancel exactly.", "Text"], Cell["Require[Simplify[realExpand\
ed[\"Pole2\"] + virtualPole2, region] === 0, \"Double poles do not \
cancel\"];\n\"double poles cancel\"", "Input"], 
  Cell["The remaining single poles are removed by the MSbar collinear \
counterterms; the table at the end of this cell lists every contribution \
separately, and their sum must vanish in each column.  With the hadronic \
convolution measure dx/x (docs/CONVENTIONS.md) the counterterm for the \
incoming quark is\n\n   + (alphaS/(2 Pi)) (1/eps) [(4 Pi)^eps / Gamma(1-eps)] \
integral dz/z Pqq(z) sigma_Born(z s, z t, u),\n\nand the Born delta function \
fixes z = w, so the convolution becomes Pqq(w)/w times the Born hard part at \
(s, v).  Pqq(w)/w = CF [(1-w)/w + 2/(1-w)]_+ with the delta coefficient 3/2.  \
The outgoing quark fragments with momentum fraction z: sigma_Born(s, t/z, \
u/z) has v -> vF = v w/(1 - v + v w) and its delta function fixes z = 1 - v + \
v w, with the Jacobian v w/(1 - v + v w)^2 for the convolution variable; the \
plus prescription at z = 1 maps to w = 1 but the lower limit of z produces \
the log v in the delta coefficient.  The photon in the lepton gives + \
(alphaEM/(2 Pi)) (1/eps) P_photon/lepton(y) times the photon-quark hard part \
at y = (1-v)/(1-vw).  Each counterterm multiplies the D-dimensional Born \
term, whose order-eps part, together with the MSbar constant log(4 Pi) - \
EulerGamma, feeds the finite part.", "Text"], Cell["incomingTuple[bornTerm_] \
:= CF bornTerm {(1 - w)/w, 2, 3/2};                  (* {regular, D0, delta} \
*)\ndw = 1 - v + v w;\njFunction[bornTerm_] := v w (bornTerm /. v -> v \
w/dw)/dw^2;              (* Born at the shifted point, with the Jacobian \
*)\nfragmentation[j_] := Module[{jEnd = Simplify[j /. w -> 1, region]},\n  \
CF/v {2 (j - jEnd)/(1 - w) - v j (1 + dw), 2 jEnd, jEnd (2 Log[v] + \
3/2)}];\nphotonKernel = (1 + (1 - yPhoton)^2)/yPhoton;                        \
       (* P_photon/lepton *)\npdfPole = alphaS/(2 Pi) \
incomingTuple[born0];\nffPole = alphaS/(2 Pi) \
fragmentation[jFunction[born0]];\nphotonPole = {alphaEM/(2 Pi) photonKernel \
alphaS/Pi photon0, 0, 0};\npoleTable = {realExpanded[\"Pole1\"], {0, 0, \
virtualPole1}, pdfPole, ffPole, photonPole};\nsinglePoles = \
Total[poleTable];\nRequire[And @@ (ExactDifference[#, 0, region] === 0 & /@ \
singlePoles), \"Single poles do not cancel\", \
singlePoles];\nGrid[Prepend[MapThread[Prepend[Map[Simplify[#, region] &, #2], \
#1] &,\n  {{\"real emission\", \"virtual\", \"incoming quark\", \"outgoing \
quark\", \"photon in lepton\", \"sum\"}, Append[poleTable, singlePoles]}],\n  \
{\"1/eps coefficient of\", \"regular\", \"D0\", \"delta\"}], Alignment -> \
Left, Dividers -> {{2 -> True}, {2 -> True, 7 -> True}}]", "Input"], 
  Cell["The finite hard coefficient", "Section"], 
  Cell["The regular parts still contain the inert symbols F1[n][zAB] etc. of \
the hypergeometric functions; they are replaced by their explicit expressions \
(logarithms and dilogarithms) here.  Finite parts of the counterterms: the \
MSbar constant times the pole coefficient, plus the order-eps Born terms (for \
the fragmentation also the order-eps Jacobian 2 j0 log(1-v+vw) from \
z^(-2eps)).  The finite coefficient is the sum of real, virtual and \
counterterm pieces in the basis {regular, D0, D1, delta}.", "Text"], 
  Cell["pdfFinite = alphaS/(2 Pi) (MSbarConstant incomingTuple[born0] + \
incomingTuple[born1]);\nffFinite = alphaS/(2 Pi) (MSbarConstant \
fragmentation[jFunction[born0]] + fragmentation[jFunction[born1] + 2 \
jFunction[born0] Log[dw]]);\nphotonFinite = {alphaEM/(2 Pi) photonKernel \
alphaS/Pi (MSbarConstant photon0 + photon1), 0, 0};\ncounterterms = {#[[1]], \
#[[2]], 0, #[[3]]} &[pdfFinite + ffFinite + photonFinite];\nfinite = \
realExpanded[\"Finite\"] + {0, 0, 0, virtualFinite} + \
counterterms;\nfiniteTable = {realExpanded[\"Finite\"], {0, 0, 0, \
virtualFinite}, {#[[1]], #[[2]], 0, #[[3]]} &[pdfFinite],\n  {#[[1]], #[[2]], \
0, #[[3]]} &[ffFinite], {#[[1]], #[[2]], 0, #[[3]]} \
&[photonFinite]};\nfiniteNames = {\"real emission\", \"virtual\", \"incoming \
quark\", \"outgoing quark\", \"photon in lepton\"};\nfinite = finite /. \
reduced[\"MasterFunctions\"];                 (* insert the explicit F1, F2 \
functions *)\nprefactor = CF alphaEM^2 eq^2 alphaS/(Pi s v \
w);\nprefactorEndpoint = prefactor /. w -> 1;          (* the D0, D1 and \
delta coefficients are defined at w = 1 *)\ncoefficients = \
Map[Collect[Expand[#], {_Log, _PolyLog}, Factor] &,\n  finite/{prefactor, \
prefactorEndpoint, prefactorEndpoint, \
prefactorEndpoint}];\nTableForm[Transpose[{ThresholdBasis, coefficients}]]", 
   "Input"], Cell["The same coefficients split into their contributions (in \
units of the prefactor; regular parts with the explicit F1, F2 functions \
inserted):", "Text"], Cell["Grid[Prepend[MapThread[Prepend[Map[Simplify[#, \
region] &, #2 /. reduced[\"MasterFunctions\"]], #1] &,\n  {finiteNames, \
Map[#/{prefactor, prefactorEndpoint, prefactorEndpoint, prefactorEndpoint} &, \
finiteTable]}],\n  {\"finite part of\", \"regular\", \"D0\", \"D1\", \
\"delta\"}], Alignment -> Left, Dividers -> {{2 -> True}, {2 -> True}}]", 
   "Input"], Cell["So sigma_NLO = born0 delta(1-w) + CF alphaEM^2 eq^2 \
alphaS/(Pi s v) [R/w + B D0 + C D1 + A delta(1-w)] with the four coefficients \
above: the regular coefficient R is quoted with the factor 1/w of the papers, \
the distributions are defined at w = 1.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Comparison with the literature", "Section"], 
  Cell["reference/HSV2015-unpolarized.wl transcribes the paper's result \
exactly as printed: its prefactor and its coefficients of 1, D0, D1 and \
delta(1-w).  The paper's prefactor contains 1/w and multiplies the \
distributions as well (and its coefficient of D0 depends on w), so the two \
forms must be compared as distributions: CanonicalDistribution turns the \
paper's form into the basis used here with the identities f(w) D0 = f(1) D0 + \
(f(w) - f(1))/(1-w) and f(w) D1 = f(1) D1 + (f(w) - f(1)) log(1-w)/(1-w).  \
The difference must vanish identically in the physical region; it is also \
evaluated numerically at three points.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Get[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"reference\", \
\"HSV2015-unpolarized.wl\"}]];\npaper = HSV2015Unpolarized[s, v, w, muR, \
alphaEM, alphaS, eq, CF];\nliterature = \
CanonicalDistribution[paper[\"Prefactor\"], paper[\"Coefficients\"], \
w];\ndifference = Table[ExactDifference[finite[[i]], literature[[i]], \
region], {i, 4}];\nnumeric = NumericDifference[finite, literature, \
PhysicalPoints, 20];\n{difference, numeric}", "Input"], 
  Cell["Save", "Section"], Cell["The result is saved before the final check, \
so that a failed comparison still leaves every ingredient on disk for \
inspection.", "Text"], Cell["result = <|\"Basis\" -> ThresholdBasis, \
\"Prefactor\" -> prefactor, \"Coefficients\" -> coefficients, \"Finite\" -> \
finite,\n  \"Born\" -> born0, \"Virtual\" -> virtualFinite, \"Real\" -> \
realExpanded[\"Finite\"], \"Counterterms\" -> counterterms,\n  \
\"LiteratureDifference\" -> difference, \"Route\" -> \
route|>;\nSaveResult[\"Unpolarized\", result, route];\nRequire[difference === \
{0, 0, 0, 0}, \"Finite coefficient differs from HSV 2015\", \
difference];\ncoefficients[[4]]", "Input"]}, WindowSize -> {1000, 800}]
